WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.380 --> 00:00:08.020
GREG BRAIL: Hi I'm
Greg Brail from Google.

00:00:08.020 --> 00:00:10.270
I want to talk today about APIs.

00:00:10.270 --> 00:00:13.150
In particular, about
how companies are using

00:00:13.150 --> 00:00:16.560
APIs to deliver new experiences
for their customers,

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:20.140
whether those our business
customers or ordinary people,

00:00:20.140 --> 00:00:24.270
and how you can actually
do that in your business.

00:00:24.270 --> 00:00:26.770
There are going to be
three parts to this talk.

00:00:26.770 --> 00:00:29.310
We're going to start with an
update about some ways in which

00:00:29.310 --> 00:00:31.610
people are using APIs today.

00:00:31.610 --> 00:00:33.210
Some ways in which
companies are using

00:00:33.210 --> 00:00:36.960
APIs to deliver new digital
experiences and in some cases,

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:40.040
even new business to
business experiences.

00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:42.540
We're also going to talk about
how you can govern and manage

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:44.730
those APIs through
the whole lifecycle,

00:00:44.730 --> 00:00:48.930
so any APIs create can actually
be useful and productive

00:00:48.930 --> 00:00:52.780
and allow all sorts of new
technologies to be created.

00:00:52.780 --> 00:00:54.780
And finally, I want to
talk about implementation

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:55.590
strategies.

00:00:55.590 --> 00:00:57.900
What is API
management, how does it

00:00:57.900 --> 00:01:03.150
fit in, how do you actually
fit an API or an API program

00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:07.360
into today's kinds of
digital architecture.

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:09.820
First, however, we want
to talk a little bit

00:01:09.820 --> 00:01:11.950
about what's important on
our mind today and some

00:01:11.950 --> 00:01:13.325
of the things that
we're thinking

00:01:13.325 --> 00:01:16.520
about a lot in today's world.

00:01:16.520 --> 00:01:19.102
First of all, we're thinking
a lot about health care.

00:01:19.102 --> 00:01:20.560
We're thinking
about being healthy.

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:22.787
We're thinking about keeping
our families healthy.

00:01:22.787 --> 00:01:25.120
We're thinking about how we're
going to get health care,

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:26.662
how are we going to
visit the doctor.

00:01:26.662 --> 00:01:29.465
Are we going to do a video
visit, an actual visit.

00:01:29.465 --> 00:01:31.090
How are we going to
pay for the doctor.

00:01:31.090 --> 00:01:33.285
What's the status
of our insurance.

00:01:33.285 --> 00:01:34.660
These are all the
kinds of things

00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:37.261
that are on our mind today.

00:01:37.261 --> 00:01:39.680
We're actually going
to talk about food.

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:41.720
We all have to eat,
and nowadays, we

00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:44.000
find ourselves asking
questions like,

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:45.260
where's dinner coming from?

00:01:45.260 --> 00:01:46.310
Are we going to cook it?

00:01:46.310 --> 00:01:47.910
Are we getting grocery delivery?

00:01:47.910 --> 00:01:49.730
Are we going to
the grocery store?

00:01:49.730 --> 00:01:51.237
Are we ordering our food?

00:01:51.237 --> 00:01:52.070
Are we calling on a?

00:01:52.070 --> 00:01:53.770
Phone are we using an app?

00:01:53.770 --> 00:01:55.610
Are we using a delivery service?

00:01:55.610 --> 00:01:58.320
There are more options than
there have ever been before

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:00.280
and a of those options
are driven by APIs.

00:02:02.832 --> 00:02:05.290
And finally, we want to talk
about how we get the things we

00:02:05.290 --> 00:02:07.240
need to run our household.

00:02:07.240 --> 00:02:09.759
How do we get those
necessities of daily life,

00:02:09.759 --> 00:02:13.060
where are we going to find them,
are there enough in the store.

00:02:13.060 --> 00:02:14.560
Can we order them online?

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:16.130
Do we need to have
them delivered?

00:02:16.130 --> 00:02:18.160
Do we need to go to
the store and get them?

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:19.660
These are all the
kinds of questions

00:02:19.660 --> 00:02:21.100
that people are asking.

00:02:21.100 --> 00:02:23.020
Retail is one of those
industries that's

00:02:23.020 --> 00:02:25.570
been going through a digital
transformation for a long time

00:02:25.570 --> 00:02:26.530
now.

00:02:26.530 --> 00:02:30.730
And APIs are an important
part of what they do, as well.

00:02:30.730 --> 00:02:33.480
So let me talk about three
companies who are actually

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:36.810
in these three particular areas
and some innovative things

00:02:36.810 --> 00:02:38.270
that they're doing with APIs.

00:02:41.550 --> 00:02:43.890
The first company is
Change Health Care.

00:02:43.890 --> 00:02:46.500
Change Health Care is not
a health care provider

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:49.050
or a hospital,
conglomerate, or a doctor.

00:02:49.050 --> 00:02:51.480
They're a service provider
to all those people.

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:52.950
In particular, they
offer something

00:02:52.950 --> 00:02:54.990
that they call the
API and Services

00:02:54.990 --> 00:02:56.730
Connection for
Health Care, which

00:02:56.730 --> 00:02:58.120
is just what it sounds like.

00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:01.560
It's a set of APIs that these
different kinds of health care

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:05.910
providers can use in order
to deliver things like health

00:03:05.910 --> 00:03:08.220
eligibility calculations.

00:03:08.220 --> 00:03:10.602
Patients' responsibility
calculations,

00:03:10.602 --> 00:03:12.310
so when you're going
to go to the doctor,

00:03:12.310 --> 00:03:14.160
you can have some
idea how much you'll

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:17.850
have to pay out of pocket versus
how much insurance will cover.

00:03:17.850 --> 00:03:19.650
So basically, they're
a service provider

00:03:19.650 --> 00:03:21.120
for all these health
care companies

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:24.390
that want to offer a better
digital patient experience.

00:03:24.390 --> 00:03:26.880
And they're using APIs
for one of the things

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:28.920
that we've talked
about APIs being useful

00:03:28.920 --> 00:03:32.730
for for many years, which is to
allow third party innovation.

00:03:32.730 --> 00:03:34.830
They provide a set of services.

00:03:34.830 --> 00:03:37.590
And their customers can
then build on those services

00:03:37.590 --> 00:03:42.790
to deliver innovative technology
for all of their consumers.

00:03:42.790 --> 00:03:45.310
Now if we move on
to food, one company

00:03:45.310 --> 00:03:48.970
that's no stranger to a
digital experiences Pizza Hut.

00:03:48.970 --> 00:03:51.700
Pizza Hut now has an app
called the pizza tracker.

00:03:51.700 --> 00:03:53.470
And the pizza tracker
literally lets

00:03:53.470 --> 00:03:55.360
you keep track of your pizza.

00:03:55.360 --> 00:03:57.550
And believe it or not,
on the Super Bowl,

00:03:57.550 --> 00:03:59.800
which is the American
football championship, which

00:03:59.800 --> 00:04:03.617
is a big event in America,
a lot of people want pizza.

00:04:03.617 --> 00:04:06.200
And when they order that pizza,
they want to know where it is.

00:04:06.200 --> 00:04:09.520
So Pizza Hut has to not only
deliver these new digital

00:04:09.520 --> 00:04:11.710
experiences-- because people
don't just want to call

00:04:11.710 --> 00:04:14.680
the local pizza place and have
someone mumble back to them

00:04:14.680 --> 00:04:17.140
and hope that their pizza
is going to arrive in time

00:04:17.140 --> 00:04:17.940
for the kickoff--

00:04:17.940 --> 00:04:19.440
they want to know
that they're going

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:22.180
to get the exact pizza they
ordered, when they ordered it,

00:04:22.180 --> 00:04:23.480
when they need it.

00:04:23.480 --> 00:04:26.830
So for that, this pizza tracker
is a new digital experience

00:04:26.830 --> 00:04:27.940
they've created.

00:04:27.940 --> 00:04:30.960
And they've built it on
top of a layer of APIs

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:32.710
that connects to systems
that they already

00:04:32.710 --> 00:04:35.505
had within the business
for tackling these things.

00:04:35.505 --> 00:04:36.880
In addition, of
course, they have

00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:38.800
to deal with these
huge traffic spikes.

00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:40.840
The Super Bowl is
probably the biggest one

00:04:40.840 --> 00:04:42.520
they've had to
deal with to date.

00:04:42.520 --> 00:04:45.400
So they take advantage of all
the things underneath the API

00:04:45.400 --> 00:04:49.060
layer and like auto scaling,
Google Kubernetes Engine,

00:04:49.060 --> 00:04:51.010
and other technologies
like that,

00:04:51.010 --> 00:04:54.160
so that when that demand
spikes, their IT systems

00:04:54.160 --> 00:04:55.990
don't struggle,
and you don't give

00:04:55.990 --> 00:04:57.610
the customers a bad experience.

00:04:57.610 --> 00:04:59.560
But then you give
them a good experience

00:04:59.560 --> 00:05:01.600
of giving them a
responsive app that

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:04.330
lets them track a very important
thing that's on their way

00:05:04.330 --> 00:05:08.440
to their house to keep
their friends happy and fed.

00:05:08.440 --> 00:05:10.262
And finally, we'll
talk about retail.

00:05:10.262 --> 00:05:13.760
Retail Has been doing digital
things for a long time.

00:05:13.760 --> 00:05:16.000
But there are a lot of new
things that are happening.

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:18.100
Conrad Electronic
is one example--

00:05:18.100 --> 00:05:19.908
they're actually in Germany.

00:05:19.908 --> 00:05:22.450
And one of the things they did
is they took a lot of the data

00:05:22.450 --> 00:05:24.550
from their existing
legacy data warehouse

00:05:24.550 --> 00:05:26.350
and moved it into BigQuery.

00:05:26.350 --> 00:05:28.060
This is not a
BigQuery presentation.

00:05:28.060 --> 00:05:30.220
There are some
great ones at NEXT

00:05:30.220 --> 00:05:31.777
I'm sure you can listen to.

00:05:31.777 --> 00:05:33.610
But one of the things
they did with BigQuery

00:05:33.610 --> 00:05:36.310
then was give
access to those data

00:05:36.310 --> 00:05:39.010
warehouse items via an API.

00:05:39.010 --> 00:05:41.380
By doing that, now
their B2B customers

00:05:41.380 --> 00:05:44.440
can now use those APIs
on top of BigQuery

00:05:44.440 --> 00:05:46.660
to have more timely
and more accurate

00:05:46.660 --> 00:05:48.640
information about
Conrad's offerings

00:05:48.640 --> 00:05:51.700
and even their inventory
than they could have before,

00:05:51.700 --> 00:05:53.860
because they gave him
a channel for getting

00:05:53.860 --> 00:05:56.110
that information that's
much easier for developers

00:05:56.110 --> 00:05:57.228
to consume.

00:05:57.228 --> 00:05:59.770
In addition-- and we'll talk a
little bit about this pattern,

00:05:59.770 --> 00:06:00.670
as well--

00:06:00.670 --> 00:06:02.830
once they had those
APIs, they could

00:06:02.830 --> 00:06:04.462
build a new in-store tool.

00:06:04.462 --> 00:06:05.920
And that in-store
tool is something

00:06:05.920 --> 00:06:07.510
that their associates
in the store

00:06:07.510 --> 00:06:10.480
can use in order to
help customers better

00:06:10.480 --> 00:06:13.510
understand what's in
stock, what the price is.

00:06:13.510 --> 00:06:15.160
And now when you
come to the store,

00:06:15.160 --> 00:06:17.140
the people who work in
the store with that tool

00:06:17.140 --> 00:06:20.200
are better informed than
they ever were before,

00:06:20.200 --> 00:06:22.900
resulting in a better customer
experience, which I imagine

00:06:22.900 --> 00:06:25.580
means more people
want to shop there.

00:06:25.580 --> 00:06:27.400
So that's a really
cool example of using

00:06:27.400 --> 00:06:30.700
an API for multiple
contexts, both for B2B,

00:06:30.700 --> 00:06:32.230
as well as to improve
the way things

00:06:32.230 --> 00:06:37.820
work internally, and improve
their own customer experience.

00:06:37.820 --> 00:06:40.030
So that's a little bit
about how APIs are being

00:06:40.030 --> 00:06:42.560
used by all kinds of companies.

00:06:42.560 --> 00:06:45.610
And they all have APIs
at the core, really.

00:06:45.610 --> 00:06:46.890
And what we found is that--

00:06:46.890 --> 00:06:51.130
I've been talking about APIs
on and off since about 2009.

00:06:51.130 --> 00:06:54.370
It began as something
that people were not sure

00:06:54.370 --> 00:06:56.230
why we were talking
about it-- like, really,

00:06:56.230 --> 00:06:59.350
do we really want to offer
these external services?

00:06:59.350 --> 00:07:01.450
And now, they've
really become the way

00:07:01.450 --> 00:07:03.340
that industries of
all types communicate.

00:07:03.340 --> 00:07:06.300
And those examples are just
some of the examples of the way

00:07:06.300 --> 00:07:08.050
that people are doing
things like building

00:07:08.050 --> 00:07:09.970
new mobile
applications, building

00:07:09.970 --> 00:07:11.680
other kinds of
digital applications,

00:07:11.680 --> 00:07:14.470
and building business to
business services based

00:07:14.470 --> 00:07:15.760
on APIs.

00:07:15.760 --> 00:07:18.550
But of course, once you build
the API, you have to govern it.

00:07:18.550 --> 00:07:20.200
You have to keep
track of it, and you

00:07:20.200 --> 00:07:22.210
have to make that API useful.

00:07:22.210 --> 00:07:25.060
And that's really where a lot of
the challenge of API management

00:07:25.060 --> 00:07:27.200
comes in.

00:07:27.200 --> 00:07:29.720
Whenever we talk
about APIs at Google,

00:07:29.720 --> 00:07:31.750
we almost always start
with this little chart,

00:07:31.750 --> 00:07:34.250
we see here, which we call
the digital value chain.

00:07:34.250 --> 00:07:36.500
And there are a couple of
important things about this.

00:07:36.500 --> 00:07:38.400
One important
thing is, of course

00:07:38.400 --> 00:07:40.250
I mentioned, is the technology.

00:07:40.250 --> 00:07:42.740
After all, an API is
a bit of technology

00:07:42.740 --> 00:07:45.530
that sits in between
the back end and an app.

00:07:45.530 --> 00:07:47.570
It's literally the
connection between the app

00:07:47.570 --> 00:07:48.740
and the backend.

00:07:48.740 --> 00:07:51.080
But an equally important
part of this value chain,

00:07:51.080 --> 00:07:53.390
maybe even more important,
is the connection

00:07:53.390 --> 00:07:54.742
between the people.

00:07:54.742 --> 00:07:57.200
And the connection that we're
especially interested in when

00:07:57.200 --> 00:07:59.600
we talk about APIs
is that connection

00:07:59.600 --> 00:08:03.980
between the developer who
uses the API and the API team

00:08:03.980 --> 00:08:07.670
or the people who create the
API or manage or govern or even

00:08:07.670 --> 00:08:10.940
are sometimes called the
product managers of the API.

00:08:10.940 --> 00:08:13.460
Because making an API
successful is more than just

00:08:13.460 --> 00:08:15.940
sticking something on the
internet with an endpoint

00:08:15.940 --> 00:08:17.480
and telling people to use it.

00:08:17.480 --> 00:08:20.810
It includes all kinds of
aspects that a product manager

00:08:20.810 --> 00:08:25.020
has to consider when they want
to make the API be useful.

00:08:25.020 --> 00:08:27.390
The interesting thing about
this digital value chain

00:08:27.390 --> 00:08:32.549
is it also lets us focus on
of where the real value is.

00:08:32.549 --> 00:08:34.710
So as technologists,
we always want

00:08:34.710 --> 00:08:36.120
to start with the
backend stuff--

00:08:36.120 --> 00:08:37.870
stuff we understand.

00:08:37.870 --> 00:08:39.659
We have a legacy
system that keeps

00:08:39.659 --> 00:08:41.640
track of our inventory for us.

00:08:41.640 --> 00:08:44.010
Let's expose it as an API.

00:08:44.010 --> 00:08:45.763
What does the API look like?

00:08:45.763 --> 00:08:47.430
Well, it looks like
our back end system.

00:08:47.430 --> 00:08:48.422
How do you use it?

00:08:48.422 --> 00:08:50.380
I don't know, do you know
our back end system--

00:08:50.380 --> 00:08:51.660
that's how you use it.

00:08:51.660 --> 00:08:53.820
That's not really
always the best

00:08:53.820 --> 00:08:56.040
way to make some
technology useful

00:08:56.040 --> 00:08:59.830
outside the organization, but
it's often where we start.

00:08:59.830 --> 00:09:01.710
But it's not always
the most effective way.

00:09:01.710 --> 00:09:03.480
What about the developers?

00:09:03.480 --> 00:09:05.880
How are they going to use
those APIs effectively?

00:09:05.880 --> 00:09:07.770
What about the actual customer?

00:09:07.770 --> 00:09:10.472
Does the API do what they need.

00:09:10.472 --> 00:09:12.180
So it makes sense in
another perspective,

00:09:12.180 --> 00:09:15.230
often, to talk about an
outside-in perspective,

00:09:15.230 --> 00:09:18.180
starting with really, what
do the customers want.

00:09:18.180 --> 00:09:20.310
If it's a business
to consumer aspect,

00:09:20.310 --> 00:09:21.870
you have product
managers, you have

00:09:21.870 --> 00:09:24.060
people in the
organization who probably

00:09:24.060 --> 00:09:26.940
have all sorts of great
ideas for new services

00:09:26.940 --> 00:09:28.860
they could offer
to their customers.

00:09:28.860 --> 00:09:31.530
Going back to that Pizza
Hut pizza tracker app--

00:09:31.530 --> 00:09:34.710
someone within Pizza Hut had
an idea to build that app.

00:09:34.710 --> 00:09:36.420
They had to come to
a technology team

00:09:36.420 --> 00:09:38.220
and say, how are we
going to build that?

00:09:38.220 --> 00:09:40.650
Where's the information
that we need to build that?

00:09:40.650 --> 00:09:43.990
Well, it may be locked inside
legacy systems in your company.

00:09:43.990 --> 00:09:47.068
And it may require some
new APIs to expose it.

00:09:47.068 --> 00:09:49.110
As another example, you
might want to do business

00:09:49.110 --> 00:09:51.040
to business like Conrad did.

00:09:51.040 --> 00:09:52.710
You might want to
actually make APIs

00:09:52.710 --> 00:09:55.590
that your large
partners can use to have

00:09:55.590 --> 00:09:58.200
a more efficient relationship
with you, which we

00:09:58.200 --> 00:09:59.970
hope will drive more business.

00:09:59.970 --> 00:10:01.998
That might drive a
different kind of API.

00:10:01.998 --> 00:10:04.290
There might not only be a
different set of requirements

00:10:04.290 --> 00:10:07.470
for what the API does, but there
could be different requirements

00:10:07.470 --> 00:10:08.620
for the technology.

00:10:08.620 --> 00:10:10.447
Maybe security has
to work differently.

00:10:10.447 --> 00:10:12.030
Maybe the data has
got to be presented

00:10:12.030 --> 00:10:13.590
in a different format.

00:10:13.590 --> 00:10:16.980
These are all questions that
a really effective API team

00:10:16.980 --> 00:10:18.900
probably ought to be
asking when they start

00:10:18.900 --> 00:10:22.420
to do something with APIs.

00:10:22.420 --> 00:10:26.140
So a great example of how to
apply this digital value chain

00:10:26.140 --> 00:10:28.930
is to start with a
real actionable project

00:10:28.930 --> 00:10:31.690
and then move on in order.

00:10:31.690 --> 00:10:33.820
We did a webinar
recently with MTN,

00:10:33.820 --> 00:10:36.920
which is a multinational
telco located in Africa.

00:10:36.920 --> 00:10:38.320
And they've embraced APIs.

00:10:38.320 --> 00:10:40.450
And I think they
have a great example

00:10:40.450 --> 00:10:42.910
for how you can actually
use this digital value

00:10:42.910 --> 00:10:44.860
chain in production.

00:10:44.860 --> 00:10:47.650
They started by solving the
problem right in front of them.

00:10:47.650 --> 00:10:50.500
They needed some specific
point to point apps.

00:10:50.500 --> 00:10:53.308
And they created APIs
to support those apps.

00:10:53.308 --> 00:10:54.850
But then they learned
that they could

00:10:54.850 --> 00:10:57.280
use those same APIs
for other things,

00:10:57.280 --> 00:11:01.390
for some of their web apps,
to drive their call centers.

00:11:01.390 --> 00:11:04.630
And then after that, they were
able to have additional pieces

00:11:04.630 --> 00:11:07.870
of functionality that they
could build with their APIs

00:11:07.870 --> 00:11:13.030
like omnichannel retail, like
APIs for use by their partners.

00:11:13.030 --> 00:11:16.750
Like even eventually, things
like digital assistance and IoT

00:11:16.750 --> 00:11:18.230
devices.

00:11:18.230 --> 00:11:20.740
All of these things
begin to be possible

00:11:20.740 --> 00:11:23.170
as they started
with a simple API

00:11:23.170 --> 00:11:25.940
and started by solving a
single business problem.

00:11:25.940 --> 00:11:28.960
And then they branched out to
build a much more comprehensive

00:11:28.960 --> 00:11:33.540
platform, where today, they
have a wide variety of APIs

00:11:33.540 --> 00:11:36.220
and a wide variety of
business processes.

00:11:36.220 --> 00:11:38.980
And this has really
enabled them to speed up

00:11:38.980 --> 00:11:42.340
the ways in which their
customers and their own product

00:11:42.340 --> 00:11:44.210
managers can create new things.

00:11:44.210 --> 00:11:46.210
And we're seeing this
example across a whole lot

00:11:46.210 --> 00:11:48.730
of industries,
where companies that

00:11:48.730 --> 00:11:53.020
used to take months to
bring a new partner on board

00:11:53.020 --> 00:11:55.060
or create a new
marketing campaign

00:11:55.060 --> 00:11:57.760
are able to do this sort
of thing in days or weeks,

00:11:57.760 --> 00:12:00.250
because once you have the
API, and once you have

00:12:00.250 --> 00:12:02.200
the technology to make
it possible for people

00:12:02.200 --> 00:12:04.900
to consume it via
self-service, the cost

00:12:04.900 --> 00:12:08.650
to get a new consumer on
that API is extremely low.

00:12:08.650 --> 00:12:11.080
Before you had APIs and
before you had self-service,

00:12:11.080 --> 00:12:12.812
it was a totally
different world.

00:12:12.812 --> 00:12:14.770
When you have to build
a completely new service

00:12:14.770 --> 00:12:17.710
or a new business or hire
software developers in order

00:12:17.710 --> 00:12:21.230
to implement a new idea, it's
much, much, much more expensive

00:12:21.230 --> 00:12:22.750
than when someone
can simply sign up

00:12:22.750 --> 00:12:24.640
for an API via
self-service using

00:12:24.640 --> 00:12:27.370
an interface that a
developer understands well

00:12:27.370 --> 00:12:30.330
and start working on.

00:12:30.330 --> 00:12:32.870
So what we find is
that the customers that

00:12:32.870 --> 00:12:37.100
succeed with APIs end up
sort of moving up this chain

00:12:37.100 --> 00:12:38.900
from the left to the right.

00:12:38.900 --> 00:12:41.450
People often start out with
APIs to solve point to point

00:12:41.450 --> 00:12:43.370
problems, and that's fine.

00:12:43.370 --> 00:12:46.400
We need to integrate system
A and system B. Let's

00:12:46.400 --> 00:12:47.660
do it with an API.

00:12:47.660 --> 00:12:49.430
So maybe once that
integration is built,

00:12:49.430 --> 00:12:51.410
we can use it in other ways.

00:12:51.410 --> 00:12:55.490
Or we need to build a
new mobile application.

00:12:55.490 --> 00:12:57.710
Let's build an API for
that mobile application,

00:12:57.710 --> 00:12:59.965
but let's make it
reusable enough

00:12:59.965 --> 00:13:02.090
that we'll be able to use
it for other applications

00:13:02.090 --> 00:13:03.127
in the future.

00:13:03.127 --> 00:13:05.210
And that's where we start
to move up to the right.

00:13:05.210 --> 00:13:08.210
We move from basically
giving access to APIs

00:13:08.210 --> 00:13:11.060
to creating those differential
digital experiences

00:13:11.060 --> 00:13:13.840
like the Pizza
Hut pizza tracker.

00:13:13.840 --> 00:13:16.190
But where the real
interesting stuff comes

00:13:16.190 --> 00:13:18.740
is when people are able to
use those APIs to create

00:13:18.740 --> 00:13:22.520
ecosystems, to create
new types of business,

00:13:22.520 --> 00:13:24.860
and to enter in
new ways for people

00:13:24.860 --> 00:13:29.540
to interact with
them as companies.

00:13:29.540 --> 00:13:31.420
So there are a couple
of different reasons

00:13:31.420 --> 00:13:33.730
why you might want
to have an ecosystem.

00:13:33.730 --> 00:13:38.560
One is simply you may be able
to unlock new lines of business.

00:13:38.560 --> 00:13:41.560
Going back, I'm not sure exactly
what lines of business Pizza

00:13:41.560 --> 00:13:44.530
Hut did, but if I look for
instance at what we talked

00:13:44.530 --> 00:13:46.780
about with Change Health Care--

00:13:46.780 --> 00:13:48.730
they created a
whole new business

00:13:48.730 --> 00:13:54.370
around offering API-related
services for health care

00:13:54.370 --> 00:13:56.520
providers to use.

00:13:56.520 --> 00:13:58.270
Those ecosystems also
help you better meet

00:13:58.270 --> 00:13:59.510
the needs of the customers.

00:13:59.510 --> 00:14:01.010
And that's a really
important thing.

00:14:01.010 --> 00:14:02.980
And they help you
discover new customers.

00:14:02.980 --> 00:14:05.350
Those are really important,
because once you've

00:14:05.350 --> 00:14:08.380
started to use an API
to build an ecosystem,

00:14:08.380 --> 00:14:10.510
you may find that some
of the best customer

00:14:10.510 --> 00:14:14.620
experiences and some of the best
new lines of business for you

00:14:14.620 --> 00:14:17.080
could come from people
who are using your APIs

00:14:17.080 --> 00:14:19.060
from outside your company.

00:14:19.060 --> 00:14:21.760
We've talked for years about
API some being something

00:14:21.760 --> 00:14:24.700
that you use to drive
third party innovation,

00:14:24.700 --> 00:14:26.860
and that remains true.

00:14:26.860 --> 00:14:28.270
And we have a
number of customers

00:14:28.270 --> 00:14:29.710
who are doing that
and doing that

00:14:29.710 --> 00:14:32.030
in some very interesting ways.

00:14:32.030 --> 00:14:34.750
One great example of this
area is Ticketmaster.

00:14:34.750 --> 00:14:37.360
Ticketmaster is
primarily US, but I

00:14:37.360 --> 00:14:39.450
know they have an
international presence.

00:14:39.450 --> 00:14:41.800
And they are very successful
at selling tickets

00:14:41.800 --> 00:14:43.550
to online events--

00:14:43.550 --> 00:14:44.540
well, real events.

00:14:44.540 --> 00:14:46.420
Now maybe at this
particular moment,

00:14:46.420 --> 00:14:49.160
there aren't as many of those
selling as there once were,

00:14:49.160 --> 00:14:50.590
but that will come back.

00:14:50.590 --> 00:14:52.600
And what Ticketmaster
did is they created

00:14:52.600 --> 00:14:56.900
an API and this has
now enabled places

00:14:56.900 --> 00:14:59.960
like retailers like
Costco, digital platforms

00:14:59.960 --> 00:15:01.340
like Facebook--

00:15:01.340 --> 00:15:04.160
you can even imagine an
airline or a hotel company

00:15:04.160 --> 00:15:06.583
saying, hey, when you
book tickets at our hotel,

00:15:06.583 --> 00:15:08.000
you might also
want to get tickets

00:15:08.000 --> 00:15:10.280
to our local plays
using Ticketmaster.

00:15:10.280 --> 00:15:12.740
And because they have
those APIs for business

00:15:12.740 --> 00:15:15.122
to business interaction,
they're actually

00:15:15.122 --> 00:15:16.580
able to create a
whole new business

00:15:16.580 --> 00:15:20.420
channels to enable them to
be embedded in someone else's

00:15:20.420 --> 00:15:22.130
digital experience.

00:15:22.130 --> 00:15:25.370
And a great example of
that is that in 2018,

00:15:25.370 --> 00:15:28.430
15% of their ticket sales
were coming this way.

00:15:28.430 --> 00:15:32.030
So they've really seen
that by creating those APIs

00:15:32.030 --> 00:15:34.760
and creating a platform
or an ecosystem, now

00:15:34.760 --> 00:15:37.970
third parties can do innovative
things with the services

00:15:37.970 --> 00:15:40.440
that they already offer.

00:15:40.440 --> 00:15:42.390
Now a key to making
all this work

00:15:42.390 --> 00:15:44.910
is what we call an
API product, which

00:15:44.910 --> 00:15:48.970
is basically to say that these
APIs are not just technology.

00:15:48.970 --> 00:15:53.100
An API is not simply a
list of your URLs and verbs

00:15:53.100 --> 00:15:57.000
and OAuth tokens and TLS
requirements and rate limits

00:15:57.000 --> 00:15:58.470
and JSON.

00:15:58.470 --> 00:16:01.300
That's the technology
behind the API.

00:16:01.300 --> 00:16:02.760
But when you build
an API, you're

00:16:02.760 --> 00:16:04.770
creating it for somebody.

00:16:04.770 --> 00:16:06.510
You're creating
it for a developer

00:16:06.510 --> 00:16:08.470
who's going to build an app.

00:16:08.470 --> 00:16:11.310
So you have to think about all
the different aspects of that.

00:16:11.310 --> 00:16:14.670
How are you going to secure
that so that the right security

00:16:14.670 --> 00:16:17.400
mechanism is used that for one,
your developers can actually

00:16:17.400 --> 00:16:19.860
understand, but then
meets the security

00:16:19.860 --> 00:16:22.260
requirements for the kind
of data that's in your API?

00:16:22.260 --> 00:16:23.760
You've got to think
about how you're

00:16:23.760 --> 00:16:25.470
going to monitor and
analyze it, so you

00:16:25.470 --> 00:16:29.020
can tell whether people are
using the API effectively,

00:16:29.020 --> 00:16:30.900
whether it's bringing
business value,

00:16:30.900 --> 00:16:33.510
whether developers
are being successful.

00:16:33.510 --> 00:16:36.150
You might even think about
monetizing your API, which

00:16:36.150 --> 00:16:38.280
is you might find cases
where it makes sense

00:16:38.280 --> 00:16:40.200
to charge for an API.

00:16:40.200 --> 00:16:42.010
That's not something
that often comes up,

00:16:42.010 --> 00:16:43.470
but there are a
number of companies

00:16:43.470 --> 00:16:46.320
who have APIs that
provide businesses

00:16:46.320 --> 00:16:48.342
where literally, on a
transactional basis,

00:16:48.342 --> 00:16:49.550
they're worth a lot of money.

00:16:49.550 --> 00:16:52.020
They provide very detailed
financial projections,

00:16:52.020 --> 00:16:53.140
for instance.

00:16:53.140 --> 00:16:56.250
That's a great example for
monetizing an API directly.

00:16:56.250 --> 00:16:58.270
We find other cases
like Ticketmaster,

00:16:58.270 --> 00:17:01.020
where it may actually not make
sense to monetize your API,

00:17:01.020 --> 00:17:03.180
because it's driving
more business to you.

00:17:03.180 --> 00:17:06.390
Or you might even want to pursue
a profit sharing philosophy,

00:17:06.390 --> 00:17:09.810
where people who are using your
API to drive business to you

00:17:09.810 --> 00:17:12.030
might actually take a
share of your profits.

00:17:12.030 --> 00:17:14.460
We've seen people do all these
kinds of things with APIs,

00:17:14.460 --> 00:17:18.839
but the key to it is to enable
self-service for developers.

00:17:18.839 --> 00:17:22.839
That's really what APIs and
API management are all about.

00:17:22.839 --> 00:17:26.160
It's not that difficult to
put the technology in place

00:17:26.160 --> 00:17:28.140
to make it possible
to call something

00:17:28.140 --> 00:17:31.530
in your business over
HTTP, send it some JSON,

00:17:31.530 --> 00:17:33.180
and get a result back.

00:17:33.180 --> 00:17:35.820
But now if you want
tens or hundreds

00:17:35.820 --> 00:17:37.830
or hundreds of
thousands of developers

00:17:37.830 --> 00:17:40.860
to use that API, either
inside your company

00:17:40.860 --> 00:17:42.708
or outside your
company, you've really

00:17:42.708 --> 00:17:45.000
got to think about how they're
going to learn about it,

00:17:45.000 --> 00:17:46.583
how they're going
to get access to it,

00:17:46.583 --> 00:17:48.780
and how they can do
this via self-service.

00:17:48.780 --> 00:17:51.030
If using your API requires
going to a meeting

00:17:51.030 --> 00:17:53.055
or attending a
conference call, it's

00:17:53.055 --> 00:17:54.930
a lot less interesting
than if it's something

00:17:54.930 --> 00:17:58.460
you can do right there in your
web browser on your computer.

00:17:58.460 --> 00:18:02.030
OK, now we talked about
some examples of using APIs.

00:18:02.030 --> 00:18:03.530
We've talked a
little bit about APIs

00:18:03.530 --> 00:18:06.260
as products and the
importance of self-service.

00:18:06.260 --> 00:18:10.220
Let's talk about how we do this
and how APIs and API management

00:18:10.220 --> 00:18:12.410
actually fit in an organization.

00:18:12.410 --> 00:18:15.260
So within an organization,
APIs can be everywhere.

00:18:15.260 --> 00:18:16.950
They can be in legacy systems.

00:18:16.950 --> 00:18:18.590
They can come from SaaS vendors.

00:18:18.590 --> 00:18:20.390
They can be third party API.

00:18:20.390 --> 00:18:22.790
Increasingly, we see
more and more people

00:18:22.790 --> 00:18:24.980
having third party
services coming over

00:18:24.980 --> 00:18:26.900
the internet that
are part of the APIs

00:18:26.900 --> 00:18:29.600
they're delivering, even
inside a corporate network

00:18:29.600 --> 00:18:31.550
or internal applications.

00:18:31.550 --> 00:18:34.610
Of course, we have VMs,
we have legacy systems,

00:18:34.610 --> 00:18:37.490
we have serverless platforms,
we have Kubernetes.

00:18:37.490 --> 00:18:39.560
We have all kinds of technology.

00:18:39.560 --> 00:18:41.450
But one thing that's
becoming increasingly

00:18:41.450 --> 00:18:43.390
clear in all sizes
of an organization

00:18:43.390 --> 00:18:47.780
is all those bits of technology
can be used to deliver APIs.

00:18:47.780 --> 00:18:51.080
And APIs can sit on all of them
and they can talk to each other

00:18:51.080 --> 00:18:54.720
and clients can talk to them and
all kinds of things can happen.

00:18:54.720 --> 00:18:57.890
And the more and more
APIs an organization has,

00:18:57.890 --> 00:19:00.560
the more important it is to
be able to keep track of them,

00:19:00.560 --> 00:19:02.690
to have some sort of
governance over them

00:19:02.690 --> 00:19:05.330
so that a product manager
or even a security person

00:19:05.330 --> 00:19:09.200
can ask her questions
like, what APIs do we have?

00:19:09.200 --> 00:19:11.810
How effectively are
they being reused?

00:19:11.810 --> 00:19:14.780
Are they secured in
the appropriate way?

00:19:14.780 --> 00:19:16.790
Which ones are the most popular?

00:19:16.790 --> 00:19:19.962
How many APIs do we have
that nobody is using?

00:19:19.962 --> 00:19:21.920
These are the kinds of
things that people bring

00:19:21.920 --> 00:19:25.580
in API management platform in
for, because by registering

00:19:25.580 --> 00:19:28.190
APIs with the API
management platform,

00:19:28.190 --> 00:19:31.370
and getting statistics and
analytics from that platform,

00:19:31.370 --> 00:19:34.100
it's actually possible to
answer some of these questions.

00:19:34.100 --> 00:19:35.900
And many organizations
find out they have

00:19:35.900 --> 00:19:37.790
APIs they didn't know about.

00:19:37.790 --> 00:19:39.680
Or they have APIs that
they do know about,

00:19:39.680 --> 00:19:41.670
but aren't being
used effectively.

00:19:41.670 --> 00:19:44.660
And this is a big important
step for customers and companies

00:19:44.660 --> 00:19:47.150
that are using APIs
internally to drive

00:19:47.150 --> 00:19:49.730
all sorts of innovation.

00:19:49.730 --> 00:19:51.800
Of course, once we
start to drive the APIs

00:19:51.800 --> 00:19:54.770
outside the organization, it
gets even more interesting,

00:19:54.770 --> 00:19:58.050
because now we not only have to
deal with what happens inside,

00:19:58.050 --> 00:20:01.670
but what happens outside adds
a few new layers of complexity.

00:20:01.670 --> 00:20:03.230
For instance, the
developer is using

00:20:03.230 --> 00:20:06.350
the API are not necessarily
part of your organization.

00:20:06.350 --> 00:20:07.850
So you need to
figure out how you're

00:20:07.850 --> 00:20:10.100
going to secure that
API for their use.

00:20:10.100 --> 00:20:12.630
Do they work with an
existing Identity Platform

00:20:12.630 --> 00:20:15.170
or do you create a
new one just for them?

00:20:15.170 --> 00:20:17.780
If developers are going to
use an API via self-service,

00:20:17.780 --> 00:20:20.930
they might be using some sort
of developer portal or website

00:20:20.930 --> 00:20:24.030
where they can learn about
APIs and sign up for access.

00:20:24.030 --> 00:20:25.370
How do you secure that?

00:20:25.370 --> 00:20:27.540
How do you maintain
identity there?

00:20:27.540 --> 00:20:30.230
How do you make sure your API
documentation is effective

00:20:30.230 --> 00:20:33.420
and your API designs are
effectively communicated?

00:20:33.420 --> 00:20:35.990
So whereas internally, API
management and governance

00:20:35.990 --> 00:20:39.800
tends to be about things like
enforcing API design standards,

00:20:39.800 --> 00:20:42.350
enforcing API
security standards,

00:20:42.350 --> 00:20:44.720
and keeping track
of what's going on,

00:20:44.720 --> 00:20:47.000
API management outside
the organization

00:20:47.000 --> 00:20:50.270
now includes things like
how do I market the API?

00:20:50.270 --> 00:20:54.330
How do I make the API easy
for developers to consume?

00:20:54.330 --> 00:20:56.670
How do I add additional
levels of security?

00:20:56.670 --> 00:20:59.790
So for instance,
in a lot of cases,

00:20:59.790 --> 00:21:02.750
people who have APIs on the
internet that don't require

00:21:02.750 --> 00:21:06.050
security credentials to use like
some retail APIs that let you

00:21:06.050 --> 00:21:09.170
get prices and store
locations, many of those

00:21:09.170 --> 00:21:11.013
are subject to attacks by bots.

00:21:11.013 --> 00:21:13.430
And these are not banks that
are doing a denial of service

00:21:13.430 --> 00:21:15.350
or trying to hack your system.

00:21:15.350 --> 00:21:17.480
These are people who have
signed up for your API

00:21:17.480 --> 00:21:19.550
like legitimate
users, but they're

00:21:19.550 --> 00:21:23.100
using it to scrape data for
their own competitive purposes.

00:21:23.100 --> 00:21:25.010
So for instance, in
the travel industry,

00:21:25.010 --> 00:21:27.380
let's try to book a hotel
room at all the hotels

00:21:27.380 --> 00:21:30.480
and see what price they can
the competition is offering.

00:21:30.480 --> 00:21:33.170
There are a lot of different
and interesting kind of security

00:21:33.170 --> 00:21:35.300
threats and other things
that a company has

00:21:35.300 --> 00:21:37.820
to take into consideration
when they offer

00:21:37.820 --> 00:21:40.410
APIs outside the organization.

00:21:40.410 --> 00:21:43.070
And one of the roles of
an API management system

00:21:43.070 --> 00:21:46.110
is to help you manage those
sorts of things, as well.

00:21:46.110 --> 00:21:48.440
So now we're not only helping
you govern and maintain

00:21:48.440 --> 00:21:50.510
standards inside
the organization,

00:21:50.510 --> 00:21:51.980
we're also helping
you effectively

00:21:51.980 --> 00:21:55.280
make sure those APIs
are used, are used well,

00:21:55.280 --> 00:21:57.860
and are used in a
secure way by people who

00:21:57.860 --> 00:21:59.790
are outside your organization.

00:21:59.790 --> 00:22:02.605
And once you share APIs
outside an organization,

00:22:02.605 --> 00:22:03.980
that's when all
of the fun things

00:22:03.980 --> 00:22:07.520
we talked about before like
governance and the ability

00:22:07.520 --> 00:22:09.800
to create an ecosystem
and the ability

00:22:09.800 --> 00:22:13.315
to enable third party innovation
really comes into play,

00:22:13.315 --> 00:22:15.440
and where you can really
do some interesting things

00:22:15.440 --> 00:22:18.510
as an organization.

00:22:18.510 --> 00:22:20.900
Now if we jump back inside
for one more second--

00:22:20.900 --> 00:22:22.370
I don't always
like to leave this

00:22:22.370 --> 00:22:24.170
without talking a little
bit about the role

00:22:24.170 --> 00:22:27.920
of internal services and
especially micro services.

00:22:27.920 --> 00:22:30.230
There has been a lot
of cases recently where

00:22:30.230 --> 00:22:33.345
people have started to
use APIs internally,

00:22:33.345 --> 00:22:34.970
and then some of the
organizations that

00:22:34.970 --> 00:22:36.610
are getting a little
bit more advanced

00:22:36.610 --> 00:22:40.070
have been talking for the last
few years about micro services.

00:22:40.070 --> 00:22:43.040
About breaking individual
bits of functionality

00:22:43.040 --> 00:22:45.620
into many, many smaller
bits that are then

00:22:45.620 --> 00:22:49.490
deployed separately that may
run on different technology

00:22:49.490 --> 00:22:50.840
platforms.

00:22:50.840 --> 00:22:53.310
That may be programmed
in different languages,

00:22:53.310 --> 00:22:56.690
but which communicate most
often using API technology,

00:22:56.690 --> 00:22:58.990
using HTTP and JSON.

00:22:58.990 --> 00:23:03.080
Or perhaps using something
like ReQL, or GRPC.

00:23:03.080 --> 00:23:05.150
All of these things
counts as APIs.

00:23:05.150 --> 00:23:06.830
And all of this
micro services stuff

00:23:06.830 --> 00:23:09.710
is a really important
step in the evolution

00:23:09.710 --> 00:23:11.810
of businesses to
become more agile

00:23:11.810 --> 00:23:13.520
and to do things more quickly.

00:23:13.520 --> 00:23:15.530
But you don't want to
end up in a situation

00:23:15.530 --> 00:23:17.900
where, as we show
here, we actually

00:23:17.900 --> 00:23:20.525
have a lot of micro services
that talk to each other,

00:23:20.525 --> 00:23:22.400
but we have a number of
ones that we outlined

00:23:22.400 --> 00:23:25.970
in red, which are actually
the micro services that

00:23:25.970 --> 00:23:28.190
talk to other micro services.

00:23:28.190 --> 00:23:30.800
And what we often find is that
when an organization is doing

00:23:30.800 --> 00:23:33.740
micro services
development at scale,

00:23:33.740 --> 00:23:38.990
a particular team really
delivers an API or a few APIs.

00:23:38.990 --> 00:23:42.230
And that API is made
visible to other teams.

00:23:42.230 --> 00:23:43.760
That team might
choose to implement

00:23:43.760 --> 00:23:46.430
that API on top of a
Java app server or they

00:23:46.430 --> 00:23:48.380
may choose to implement
it on a mainframe.

00:23:48.380 --> 00:23:49.880
Or they might choose
it to implement

00:23:49.880 --> 00:23:53.990
with five microservices or
10 micro services or 100

00:23:53.990 --> 00:23:55.590
micro services.

00:23:55.590 --> 00:23:59.240
But for that team, to expose all
hundreds of those microservices

00:23:59.240 --> 00:24:02.420
as they are directly to all
the other teams in the company

00:24:02.420 --> 00:24:05.090
is a recipe for
disaster and chaos.

00:24:05.090 --> 00:24:07.970
So what we often find, a
pattern that works very well,

00:24:07.970 --> 00:24:11.930
is to create APIs facades for
those groups as micro services.

00:24:11.930 --> 00:24:15.360
Expose that API to the other
parts of the organization.

00:24:15.360 --> 00:24:18.120
And then what's underneath
the API can change.

00:24:18.120 --> 00:24:20.590
It might be that
monolithic Java app today

00:24:20.590 --> 00:24:24.890
and it might be 100 applications
using GRPC and GoLang tomorrow.

00:24:24.890 --> 00:24:27.200
But the interface
for that service

00:24:27.200 --> 00:24:30.620
in that team to the rest of the
organization doesn't change.

00:24:30.620 --> 00:24:32.270
That becomes a real
important principle

00:24:32.270 --> 00:24:34.370
that companies can
use as they start

00:24:34.370 --> 00:24:36.620
to explore some of the
new and more agile ways

00:24:36.620 --> 00:24:41.820
to use APIs and micro
services, technology.

00:24:41.820 --> 00:24:44.120
And finally, I want
to talk about Google

00:24:44.120 --> 00:24:45.920
and some of our offerings.

00:24:45.920 --> 00:24:47.870
We've been working
hard for a long time

00:24:47.870 --> 00:24:51.290
to offer a wide variety of
products in the API and API

00:24:51.290 --> 00:24:53.120
management space.

00:24:53.120 --> 00:24:55.310
On one side of the
slide, we see Apigee.

00:24:55.310 --> 00:24:57.380
Apigee is the API
management product

00:24:57.380 --> 00:24:59.510
we've offered for
a few years now.

00:24:59.510 --> 00:25:02.000
I've been working
on Apigee since 2007

00:25:02.000 --> 00:25:05.720
and it's been part of
Google Cloud since 2016.

00:25:05.720 --> 00:25:08.810
We have a new product as
well called API Gateway.

00:25:08.810 --> 00:25:10.880
It's a fully
managed API gateway.

00:25:10.880 --> 00:25:13.250
And in its current
form, it's especially

00:25:13.250 --> 00:25:15.520
well-suited for turning
serverless workloads

00:25:15.520 --> 00:25:17.240
like that forest
of micro services

00:25:17.240 --> 00:25:20.660
we just saw into
a coherent APIs.

00:25:20.660 --> 00:25:23.060
And we find that in many
cases, people will really

00:25:23.060 --> 00:25:25.780
want to take advantage
of this API gateway,

00:25:25.780 --> 00:25:29.310
because it's small, it's
fast, it's easy to consume,

00:25:29.310 --> 00:25:31.550
and it's fully native
and fully managed

00:25:31.550 --> 00:25:33.590
as part of Google Cloud.

00:25:33.590 --> 00:25:35.390
Apigee has many of
those attributes,

00:25:35.390 --> 00:25:39.620
but Apigee is also capable of
full lifecycle API management,

00:25:39.620 --> 00:25:43.160
managing everything in your
API program from the definition

00:25:43.160 --> 00:25:46.550
of APIs to publishing
on developer portals

00:25:46.550 --> 00:25:49.340
to monetization and
advanced analytics.

00:25:49.340 --> 00:25:51.620
And over time, you'll see
us adding more capabilities

00:25:51.620 --> 00:25:53.420
to both of these
products in order

00:25:53.420 --> 00:25:55.790
to give it a really
good set of capabilities

00:25:55.790 --> 00:25:59.330
so that our customers with
wide varieties of API needs

00:25:59.330 --> 00:26:03.760
can find the right
solution in Google Cloud.

00:26:03.760 --> 00:26:05.467
So finally, thank you.

00:26:05.467 --> 00:26:08.050
Thank you on behalf of everyone
at Google Cloud for joining me

00:26:08.050 --> 00:26:08.890
today.

00:26:08.890 --> 00:26:11.320
And may you have some really
awesome digital journeys

00:26:11.320 --> 00:26:13.050
in the future.

